\startchapter[reference=methods, title=Methods]

\startsection[title={BART-Seq (demultiplexing and quantification)}]

\TODO[Is the RNA-Seq explanation in the intro sufficient for this chapter?]
BART-Seq is a targeted RNA sequencing protocol able to sensitively and inexpensively measure expressions of thousands of samples \cite[uzbas2019].
It consists of three steps; design, experiment, and quantification.

% primer/barcode design
The design step involves algorithms to design primer pairs matching amplicons – such as genes – and barcodes identifying samples – such as single cells or cell groups.
The primer design algorithm optimizes primer pairs to stably dimerize with their target amplicons.
The barcode design algorithm minimizes the chance of misassigning barcodes through sequencing errors by maximizing their mutual alignment distances.
The set of barcodes is split into two halves arbitrarily – forward and reverse.
The results are the sequences for two sets of barcodes, two linkers, and one primer pair per amplicon.

% experiment
For the experimental step, barcodes are synthesized and those of each half are ligated to the half’s respective primer.
Each well of a sequencing plate receives a different combination of forward and reverse barcode-linker-primer assemblies, allowing to address $(\text{\#barcodes} ÷ 2)^2$ wells.
The experiment concludes like a regular paired-end sequencing run \cite[fullwood2009], resulting in a raw read library containing sequences of barcode-linker-read.
\TODO[understandable? “read” is a fragment later aligned to amplicons, while “raw read” is the whole thing and only has to carry us to the next line]

% quantification: sequencing and multiplexing
In the quantification step, the barcodes, linkers and low-quality bases are cut off the raw read sequence.
The shortened read sequences are then tagged with their corresponding designed barcode if possible.
This process assigns barcodes with up to one mismatching base using the algorithm of \inlinecite[aho1975], as long as the assignment is still unique – reads that could be tagged with one of multiple barcodes that are one mismatch away from each other are discarded.
The tagged reads are then aligned to the targeted amplicons using HISAT2 \cite[kim2019].
Amplicons are counted per barcode combination according to one of two strategies.
The conservative strategy only counts where both reads aligned to the same amplicon, while the sensitive strategy also counts when one of both is unaligned.
Neither strategy counts one of the aligned amplicons when there is a mismatch.
The count of overall full matches, half matches, and mismatches is included in the various statistics collected about the demultiplexing and quantification process.

I designed the quantification step in collaboration with Fatma Uzbas and Micha Drucker, and implemented a Python library and pipeline executing it.

\stopsection

\startsection[title={Dimensionality Reduction Methods}]

Dimensionality reduction methods embed high dimensional data into a low dimensional space.
They are designed around the resulting space’s purpose, which includes 2-3 dimensional spaces for visualization or a latent space for further processing.
Some methods also assign scores to each result dimension, which can e.\,g. be used to decide which dimensions to discard and which ones to keep for visualization or as latent space.

In the context of scRNA-Seq data, the high dimensional data are gene expression matrices $x_{gc}$ with tens of thousands of genes $g∈\{1,…,G\}$, measured for up to approximately one million cells $c∈\{1,…,C\}$ at once.
%Matrices this big are very sparse due to the ubiquity of high dropout in protocols able to capture this many cells.
%\TODO[cite. only dropout makes them sparse?]
The intrinsic dimensionality of transcriptomic data is low when compared to these numbers of genes, so an embedding $s_{pc}$ with a comparatively low number of dimensions $p∈\{1,…,P\}$ is enough to capture nearly all biologically relevant variation and structure in the data.
As an example of such a latent space, about $50≤P≤100$ linear combinations of genes are sufficient to capture all information necessary to reconstruct all known biological processes \cite[ding2018].
Global structures of the data such as strongly differing cell types or differentiation can often be faithfully reflected in a 2 or 3 dimensional embedding.
Using such embeddings as scatter plot coordinates serves as the basis of visualizations interpretable by humans.

\startsubsection[title={Spectral decomposition}]

Spectral decomposition is the act of transforming the data into components that each capture a different source of variance in the data.
These components are scored by their ability to explain the data’s structure.
By keeping components with high enough scores as embedding dimensions, spectral decompositions can therefore be used as dimensionality reduction methods.
This is most useful as a preprocessing step to define a less noisy latent space reflecting the inherent dimensionality of the data, but components can also be directly visualized.

\startsubsubsection[title={Principal Component Analysis}]
Principal component analysis (PCA) is a simple spectral decomposition.
Its principal components (PCs) are weighted products of input features (such as transcripts), scored by the variance each PC explains.
As each PC ideally represents one source of variation, data with up to around 4 linearly separable sources of variation can be interpreted by visualizing the corresponding number of PCs.
As mentioned before as an example, while the intrinsic dimensionality of transcriptomic data is low, the number of PCs necessary to faithfully represent all biological processes one might care about is around 50-100 \cite[lenz2016].
Picking a number of PCs in this range, for example using the jackstraw algorithm \cite[chung2015] is a popular a preprocessing step for downstream analysis.

There are a number of related methods that can serve a similar purpose, such as independent component analysis \cite[bell1995] or factor analysis \cite[stephenson1935].
\TODO[I don’t use them but mention them for completeness, is that wrong?]
Furthermore, single cell transcriptomics specific extensions exist, such as ZINB-WaVE \cite[risso2018], which addresses zero inflation and over-dispersion of scRNASeq-data in its dimensionality reduction.
\stopsubsubsection

\startsubsubsection[title={Diffusion Maps}]
Diffusion maps provide another spectral decomposition of data.
Their practicality for data analysis was first shown by \inlinecite[coifman2005].
A diffusion map preserves global structures and the pseudo-temporal ordering of cells better than even than learned embeddings like t-SNE (see \in{section}[sec:learned-embeddings]) \cite[haghverdi2015], and the first diffusion components are often sufficient to directly capture and visualize bifurcation events in differentiating cells.

